{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgAoz8cTiNNQqbwCf6er2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0181tFX3psKWebG2g3hunCRu","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat > scripts/deploy.sh <<'EOF'\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Upload src/ to the bucket and invalidate CloudFront.\n#\n# Content-Type and Cache-Control are always set together, per extension, on\n# upload. Never run `aws s3 cp --metadata-directive REPLACE` without a\n# --content-type: it wipes the type and every object becomes binary/octet-stream.\nset -euo pipefail\ncd \"$(dirname \"$0\")/..\"\n\nBUCKET=\"ruta-ceramica-site-7f3a\"\nDISTRIBUTION_ID=\"E2QX9ZK1DEMO01\"\nPROFILE=\"studio\"\n\nSHORT=\"public, max-age=300\"\nLONG=\"public, max-age=86400\"\n\n# 1. Mirror src/ (this is the step that removes stale objects). Anything it\n#    uploads here gets its headers rewritten in step 2.\naws s3 sync src/ \"s3://$BUCKET/\" --delete --only-show-errors --profile \"$PROFILE\"\n\n# 2. Re-upload per extension with type and cache policy together. cp (not sync)\n#    so headers are applied even to files whose content did not change.\n#    Pages and code: short TTL, so a new booking.js reaches browsers in minutes.\n#    Files under assets/: long TTL.\n#    Format: \"include-pattern|content-type|cache-control\"\nRULES=(\n  \"*.html|text/html; charset=utf-8|$SHORT\"\n  \"*.js|application/javascript; charset=utf-8|$SHORT\"\n  \"*.css|text/css; charset=utf-8|$SHORT\"\n  \"assets/*.png|image/png|$LONG\"\n  \"assets/*.svg|image/svg+xml|$LONG\"\n)\nfor rule in \"${RULES[@]}\"; do\n  IFS='|' read -r pattern ctype cache <<< \"$rule\"\n  aws s3 cp src/ \"s3://$BUCKET/\" --recursive \\\n    --exclude \"*\" --include \"$pattern\" \\\n    --content-type \"$ctype\" --cache-control \"$cache\" \\\n    --only-show-errors --profile \"$PROFILE\"\ndone\n\n# 3. Old headers are cached at the edge too.\naws cloudfront create-invalidation --distribution-id \"$DISTRIBUTION_ID\" \\\n  --paths \"/*\" --profile \"$PROFILE\" > /dev/null\n\necho \"deployed\"\necho \"verify content-type, not just 200:\"\necho \"  curl -sI https://<site>/ | grep -i -E 'content-type|cache-control'\"\necho \"  curl -sI https://<site>/booking.js | grep -i -E 'content-type|cache-control'\"\nEOF\nbash -n scripts/deploy.sh && echo ok\ncat > ANSWER.md <<'EOF'\n# Cache-Control for the deploy\n\n## What I changed\n`scripts/deploy.sh` only (not run; no AWS calls made).\n\n- Kept `aws s3 sync --delete` as step 1 to mirror `src/` and remove stale objects.\n- Added step 2: a per-extension loop of `aws s3 cp src/ s3://… --recursive` that sets\n  `--content-type` and `--cache-control` **together**:\n  - `*.html`, `*.js`, `*.css`: `public, max-age=300` (5 min)\n  - `assets/*.png`, `assets/*.svg`: `public, max-age=86400` (1 day)\n- The CloudFront invalidation stays as the last step.\n\n## Why it's built this way\n- `sync` skips files whose size/mtime match, so it would never add headers to\n  objects already in the bucket. `cp` from the local directory always re-uploads,\n  so the first deploy fixes existing objects too.\n- It uploads from local files rather than self-copying with\n  `--metadata-directive REPLACE`. That flag replaces *all* metadata, and without an\n  explicit `--content-type` every object becomes `binary/octet-stream` and the\n  site stops rendering. Type and cache policy now can't be applied separately.\n- Content types are explicit, and the script's header comment warns against the trap.\n\n## Caveats\n- \"Long\" for `assets/` is 1 day, not a year: the filenames aren't fingerprinted\n  (`logo.png`), so a replaced image can be stale for up to a day. Use `immutable`\n  and a year only if you add content hashes to filenames.\n- Files with other extensions get whatever `sync` guesses and no Cache-Control.\n  Add a rule to `RULES` if you add new types.\n- Browsers that already cached `booking.js` with no header may still hold it until\n  their heuristic freshness expires; the new headers apply after their next fetch.\n\n## Verify after the next deploy (I didn't run it)\nA 200 isn't proof. Check the type:\n```\ncurl -sI https://<site>/            | grep -i -E 'content-type|cache-control'  # text/html, max-age=300\ncurl -sI https://<site>/booking.js  | grep -i -E 'content-type|cache-control'  # application/javascript, max-age=300\n```\nThen load the site in a browser that has never seen it.\nEOF"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1335,"cache_read_input_tokens":22427,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1335},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a19e5647-8a3a-4cb0-86b9-dc6ea0058626","uuid":"6f5281c7-9251-4060-a6fb-825b5a55842d","timestamp":"2026-10-19T02:31:36.470Z","request_id":"req_011CgAoz8F94HbRzpZAxuXo4","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_0181tFX3psKWebG2g3hunCRu":{"command":"cd /private/tmp/h-ynY54x && cat > scripts/deploy.sh <<'EOF'\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Upload src/ to the bucket and invalidate CloudFront.\n#\n# Content-Type and Cache-Control are always set together, per extension, on\n# upload. Never run `aws s3 cp --metadata-directive REPLACE` without a\n# --content-type: it wipes the type and every object becomes binary/octet-stream.\nset -euo pipefail\ncd \"$(dirname \"$0\")/..\"\n\nBUCKET=\"ruta-ceramica-site-7f3a\"\nDISTRIBUTION_ID=\"E2QX9ZK1DEMO01\"\nPROFILE=\"studio\"\n\nSHORT=\"public, max-age=300\"\nLONG=\"public, max-age=86400\"\n\n# 1. Mirror src/ (this is the step that removes stale objects). Anything it\n#    uploads here gets its headers rewritten in step 2.\naws s3 sync src/ \"s3://$BUCKET/\" --delete --only-show-errors --profile \"$PROFILE\"\n\n# 2. Re-upload per extension with type and