{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgAp1Az8DuuvMo8Vx5BA9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I changed `scripts/deploy.sh` so every upload carries a Cache-Control header, and wrote up what I did in `ANSWER.md`. I haven't run the deploy or made any AWS calls. The script only passed a `bash -n` syntax check, so the headers themselves are untested.\n\n- **Cache lifetimes:** HTML, JS and CSS get `public, max-age=300` (5 minutes). PNG and SVG files under `assets/` get `public, max-age=86400` (1 day).\n- **Content types:** each rule sets Content-Type and Cache-Control in the same command, so they can't drift apart. Existing objects get the headers on the next deploy, because the upload re-sends every file even if its content hasn't changed.\n- **Metadata trap:** it uploads from the local `src/` folder rather than copying objects onto themselves. A self-copy with `--metadata-directive REPLACE` and no `--content-type` is what turned every file into `binary/octet-stream` in your story. A comment in the script warns against it.\n- **Unchanged parts:** `sync --delete` still runs first to remove stale files, and the CloudFront invalidation still runs last.\n\nI used 1 day, not a year, for `assets/` because the filenames aren't fingerprinted. A replaced `logo.png` could look stale for up to a day. Moving to a year with `immutable` would need content hashes in the filenames.\n\nFiles with other extensions get whatever type `sync` guesses and no Cache-Control. Add a line to `RULES` in the script if you add new file types.\n\nAfter the next deploy, run `curl -sI` on the site root and on `booking.js` and check that the `content-type` is `text/html` and `application/javascript`. A 200 alone doesn't prove the page works."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2064,"cache_read_input_tokens":23762,"cache_creation":{"ephe